   "text": "The drawing looks like an ertugliflozin analogue, so I built the SMILES from the known ertugliflozin SMILES and changed the one substituent that differs. I did not independently re-derive the ring stereocentres from the wedges and hashes. I assumed they match ertugliflozin.\n\n- **Aryl part:** a 4-chloro-3-(4-ethoxybenzyl)phenyl group (ethoxy on one ring, a CH2 bridge, then a chloro-substituted ring) is attached to the ketal carbon of the bicycle.\n- **Bicycle:** a 6,8-dioxabicyclo[3.2.1]octane core with three OH groups on the carbocyclic ring, as in ertugliflozin.\n- **Difference from ertugliflozin:** the bridgehead carbon bears a CH(OH)CH3 group instead of CH2OH.\n- **Side-chain stereocentre:** the CH3 is on a wedge, the OH is on a plain bond, and the carbon is attached to the bridgehead. Using the priority order OH > bridgehead C > CH3, with H pointing away, this centre is R. That corresponds to `[C@@H](C)O` in the SMILES.\n\nSMILES: CCOC1=CC=C(C=C1)CC2=C(C=CC(=C2)[C@]34[C@@H]([C@H]([C@@H]([C@@](O3)(CO4)[C@@H](C)O)O)O)O)Cl"
  }
